  "read": {
    "headline": "Every Treasury from three years out yields 5%. The two-year is 8bp away.",
    "standfirst": "Monday was a rates day with an equity casualty: the ten-year rose 7.6bp to 5.24%, its highest close since 2007, the thirty-year to 5.56%, and the S&P 500 fell 0.77% to 7,683.69, its lowest in ten sessions, with the VIX up 8.07% to 16.07. Eight of eleven sectors fell; energy, staples and health care held. Gold is $4,154 after a 3% fall to a seven-week low. The Brent row shows −4.76% and it is not a sell-off: the November contract expires on Wednesday and December, about $6 lower, has taken its place in the source table while November itself trades near $106. Overnight the RBA hiked to 4.60%, the Nikkei lost 1.36% and the Kospi 1.25%, and the dollar sits near a two-month high. The board is Monday's New York close for US assets and live for everything else.",
    "points": [
      {
        "title": "The curve moved as one block, and the block is above 5%.",
        "text": "The two-year is 4.92%, up 13.36% over a month; the ten-year 5.24%, up 10.78%; the thirty-year 5.56%. Every maturity from three years out yields 5% or more and the three-month bill, at 4.28%, is the only point that moved less than 5bp on Monday, so 3M10Y is 96bp, the widest of the cycle. In the funds that is the 20-year-plus Treasury down 0.88% on the day and 12.28% over twelve months, the 7–10 year fund down 7.22% on the year, investment-grade credit down 8.16%, municipals down 6.07% year to date. Every bond row is red on every window bar the 1–3 year fund's 0.02% on the week. Two Fed governors spoke on Monday — Cook said inflation pressure will persist for months, Barkin that supply shocks are no longer temporary — and October went back to about 70% priced."
      },
      {
        "title": "Brent −4.76% is a calendar, not a crash.",
        "text": "The source table's Brent row has rolled from the November contract to December ahead of Wednesday's expiry, and December trades about $6 under November: $100.27 against a November contract near $106 this morning. That is backwardation, the premium the market pays for barrels now, and it is the same artefact WTI produced last week when its five-day column read −7.79%. WTI itself is $94.31, up 1.85% on the day; heating oil rose 0.78% and is up 114.54% year to date; gasoline is down 10.82% over five days and natural gas 5.31%. Energy was the only sector to rise on Monday, by 0.10%, and is up 37.54% over twelve months. Read the energy rows against the physical market this week, not against each other."
      },
      {
        "title": "Communication services lost 2.47% on the week. Meta did most of it.",
        "text": "Communication services fell 1.58% on Monday and 2.47% over five days, with Meta down 4.79% on the day; consumer discretionary fell 1.41% with Tesla down 3.94%; financials 1.19%; technology 0.89% despite Nvidia rising 1.68% on a $150 billion buyback expansion. Utilities fell 0.66% and are down 9.83% over twelve months, the worst sector on that window, with discretionary at −9.45% and communication services at −6.22% the other two under water. Health care rose 0.33% and is up 26.02% on the year; staples rose 0.27%. Among the factors, quality beat the index by 0.42% and low volatility and small-cap growth by 0.28% — a defensive day inside a down market — while recent IPOs lagged by 1.42% and private equity by 0.70%, taking its twelve-month shortfall to 34.00%."
      },
      {
        "title": "Emerging markets fell harder than the S&P 500, again, and for the same reason.",
        "text": "South Africa fell 3.02% on Monday, Turkey 2.58%, Argentina 2.39% with the Merval down 3.28%, Korea 1.92%, Brazil 1.67%, India 1.61%; the EM ETF lost 1.15% against the S&P 500 ETF's 0.74%. The dollar index is near a two-month high and up about 1.8% on the month; the peso is 5.81% weaker over a month, the rand 1.65%. India is now down 12.88% year to date in the ETF and the Nifty closed below 23,000 on Monday for the first time since April. Hong Kong, up 0.67%, and Australia, up 0.25% into a rate hike, were the only Asian rows to rise. This morning the Kospi is down 1.25% and 3.92% on the week, the Nikkei 1.36%, Hang Seng 0.96%; Korea is still up 128.79% over twelve months in dollars."
      },
      {
        "title": "Gold at $4,154 is a seven-week low, and it is losing to a 5.24% ten-year, not to the dollar.",
        "text": "Gold fell 3% on Monday to its lowest since 5 August and is down 3.36% over five days, 8.30% over a month and 5.31% year to date, while still 7.75% higher over twelve months. Silver at $60.69 is down 5.17% on the week and 16.63% on the year; platinum is down 20.11% year to date. The dollar's monthly gain is under 2%; the ten-year's is over 50bp. A metal that pays nothing has to compete with a Treasury that pays 5.24%, and this month it has not. Copper rose 0.21% and is up 35.49% over twelve months, still the one metal on the board that has worked, as an industrial input."
      },
      {
        "title": "The yen is the one major the dollar lost to this week. Tokyo and Washington both wanted it that way.",
        "text": "USD/JPY is 157.35, down 0.93% over five days and 3.18% over three months, after a week of coordinated warnings — Trump to Takaichi, Katayama with Bessent, and Japan's top currency diplomat telling markets on Monday to heed the signal; 160 is treated as the line. The euro is down 2.01% over a month and 3.38% on the year, near a three-month low after Lagarde promised measured steps; sterling is off 2.29% over a month. The dollar gained 3.02% on the franc over a month and 1.64% on the peso over five days; the Australian dollar fell 0.37% on the day into a hike that was fully priced. In crypto, Bitcoin at $83,268 is down 0.26% and 27.19% over twelve months; Chainlink is up 12.24% on the week and 106.32% over three months, and Litecoin has given back 5.37% of a 41.99% month."
      }
    ]
  },
